# Selecto Strict Mode Workbook

This workbook exercises the governed query boundary added in Selecto 0.4.10.
It needs no database: every example uses an explicit compile-only runtime.

## Setup

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install_core!()
import ExUnit.Assertions
```

## 1) Compose and validate the trusted domain

Strict mode begins after the application has assembled and validated its
domain. Domain-declared joins and SQL-backed compatibility fields remain
available by governed name; the query caller cannot rewrite their structure.

```elixir
strict_domain = %{
  name: "Strict orders",
  source: %{
    source_table: "orders",
    primary_key: :id,
    fields: [:id, :status, :total, :team_id],
    redact_fields: [],
    columns: %{
      id: %{type: :integer},
      status: %{type: :string},
      total: %{type: :decimal},
      team_id: %{type: :integer}
    },
    associations: %{
      team: %{queryable: :team, field: :team, owner_key: :team_id, related_key: :id}
    }
  },
  schemas: %{
    team: %{
      source_table: "teams",
      primary_key: :id,
      fields: [:id, :name],
      redact_fields: [],
      columns: %{id: %{type: :integer}, name: %{type: :string}},
      associations: %{}
    }
  },
  joins: %{
    team: %{name: "Team", type: :left, display_field: :name}
  },
  custom_columns: %{
    "status_upper" => %{select: "UPPER(selecto_root.status)", type: :string}
  }
}

strict_query = Selecto.configure(strict_domain, Selecto.Runtime.Context.new(SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter, :compile_only), mode: :strict)
assert Selecto.Policy.strict?(strict_query)
```

## 2) Compile an ordinary governed query

The join and custom column came from the configured domain. The filter value stays
in the parameter list.

```elixir
query =
  strict_query
  |> Selecto.join(:team)
  |> Selecto.select(["status", "team.name", "status_upper"])
  |> Selecto.filter({"status", "open"})
  |> Selecto.order_by({"team.name", :asc})

:ok = Selecto.Policy.validate_query!(query)
{sql, params} = Selecto.to_sql(query)

%{sql: sql, params: params, parameterized?: params == ["open"]}
```

## 3) Observe eager query-authored SQL rejection

Strict mode rejects raw SQL at the public API and checks again at compilation.
The example captures the expected exception so the notebook remains runnable.

```elixir
raw_sql_rejection =
  try do
    strict_query |> Selecto.select({:raw_sql, "current_user"})
    :unexpectedly_allowed
  rescue
    error in Selecto.PolicyViolation -> Exception.message(error)
  end

assert raw_sql_rejection =~ "query-authored :raw_sql"
raw_sql_rejection
```

## 4) Observe governed join enforcement

A caller may enable `:team`, but cannot change its type, source, keys, or
condition. Joins absent from the domain are rejected as well.

```elixir
join_override_rejection =
  try do
    strict_query |> Selecto.join(:team, type: :inner)
    :unexpectedly_allowed
  rescue
    error in Selecto.PolicyViolation -> Exception.message(error)
  end

undeclared_join_rejection =
  try do
    strict_query
    |> Selecto.join(:audit_log,
      source: "audit_logs",
      owner_key: :id,
      related_key: :order_id
    )

    :unexpectedly_allowed
  rescue
    error in Selecto.PolicyViolation -> Exception.message(error)
  end

assert undeclared_join_rejection =~ "not declared by the domain"
refute join_override_rejection == :unexpectedly_allowed
%{override: join_override_rejection, undeclared: undeclared_join_rejection}
```

## 5) Know what strict mode checks: the query, not the host's configuration

Strict mode governs what a query caller adds. It no longer fingerprints the
configured domain and compares it on every compile (certification
specification 2.38.0 removed that seal). Compilation reads the configuration
`Selecto.configure/3` built, so the edit below neither changes the generated SQL
nor raises. To change a domain, configure it again, where validation and the
`domain_sql` profile run. The query struct is host data; do not hand it to
untrusted code.

```elixir
edited_query = put_in(strict_query.domain[:source][:source_table], "other_orders")
{edited_sql, []} = edited_query |> Selecto.select(["id"]) |> Selecto.to_sql()
assert edited_sql =~ "from orders selecto_root"
refute edited_sql =~ "other_orders"
edited_sql
```

## 6) Choose the domain-SQL profile deliberately

The default `domain_sql: :declared` permits trusted SQL already in the configured
domain. `domain_sql: :forbid` rejects SQL-bearing domain entries during
configuration.

```elixir
forbid_domain_sql_rejection =
  try do
    Selecto.configure(strict_domain, Selecto.Runtime.Context.new(SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter, :compile_only),
      mode: :strict,
      domain_sql: :forbid
    )

    :unexpectedly_allowed
  rescue
    error in Selecto.PolicyViolation -> Exception.message(error)
  end

assert forbid_domain_sql_rejection =~ "domain_sql: :forbid"
forbid_domain_sql_rejection
```

Strict mode is a governance boundary, not a BEAM process sandbox. Pair it with
least-privilege database roles, tenant scoping, and database row-level security
when those controls are part of the threat model.
